# Session 7 — the correction key.  mrn-verify key.mrv
#
# One assertion per line. Three kinds of evidence, in increasing order of what they prove, and
# the order matters more than the count:
#
#   1. the MODEL — what Marionnet knows by construction. Always true if the lab was handed out
#      as it was built; worth asserting because a student may have moved a cable;
#   2. the TRACE — what the boot scenario did, and whether anything in it failed. It proves a
#      command was CALLED, never that the machine is in a given state (a trace does not show
#      redirections, and a scenario can be replaced after the boot);
#   3. the STATE and the EXPERIMENT — what the guest says about itself now (`report'), and what
#      actually crosses the network (`reaches'). Only the last one proves connectivity: a
#      report describes a state, never an accessibility.
#
# Play it against a deliberately wrong copy too. A key that passes on everything marks nothing.

# ---- 1. the model: the topology of the statement ---------------------------------------------
field r1 port_no is 3
cable c1 m1:eth0       h1:port1
cable c2 r1:eth0       h1:port2
cable c3 r1:eth2       h3:port1
cable c4 intruder:eth0 h3:port2

# ---- the session is alive (a copy shut down is marked with --refresh=never) --------------------
state m1 is on
state r1 is on
state intruder is on

# ---- 2. the trace: the configuration ran, and nothing in it failed ----------------------------
journal r1 rc_config ok

# ---- 3. the state, taken inside the guest -----------------------------------------------------
report r1 says ~ ip_forward *= *1
report r1 says ~ ^:FORWARD DROP
report r1 says ~ ^-A POSTROUTING .*MASQUERADE
report m1 says ~ default via 192\.168\.1\.254

# ---- 3. the experiment: the only proof of connectivity ----------------------------------------
reaches m1 intruder

# ---- after an exam session: what the shutdown archived ----------------------------------------
# Uncomment when marking a copy that has been shut down properly. The terminal recording is the
# one document a mark may rest on: it is written by the host, not by the guest.
# documents m1 has ~ Terminal of
